# Cloudflare Pages reads this from the OUTPUT directory, which for this project
# is the site root itself (output `.`, root directory `sailing`). It is not served.
#
# Rule order matters: Pages applies every matching rule, and a later one wins on
# a repeated header name. Keep the broad rule first and the narrow ones after.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

# Everything under assets/ is either content-hashed or a logo that changes by
# being replaced, so a year is safe. `immutable` tells the browser not to
# revalidate at all - that is the half that actually removes the request.
#
# NOTE for story #11: this covers assets/photos/ too. The trip-log derivatives
# are written once per photo and never edited in place, so that is correct - but
# if a photo is ever re-encoded under the same filename, a year is a long time
# to be wrong. Rename rather than overwrite.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The HTML itself must not be cached for a year, or a deploy would not reach
# anyone who had already visited. This is the rule the one above would otherwise
# swallow if a page ever lived under assets/.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
